# Project Updates & Issues Faced

## 1. Initial Motor Control

* Started with basic motor control and differential-drive logic.
* Faced issues with incorrect motor direction and GPIO assignments.
* Tested individual motors and corrected the left/right motor connections and control logic.

## 2. ESP8266 Wi-Fi Communication

* Initially worked with ESP8266 for Wi-Fi-based communication.
* Faced problems with Wi-Fi connection and sending control data.
* Implemented Wi-Fi communication and verified data transfer between the controller and rover.

## 3. ESP32 Migration

* The project was later changed from ESP8266 to ESP32 for both the remote and rover.
* Updated the GPIO pin assignments and motor-control code to match the ESP32.
* Modified the communication code to work with the ESP32 hardware.

## 4. Controller/Rover Communication

* Initially experimented with different communication methods.
* ESP-NOW was tested but encountered reliability and connection issues.
* Switched to a Wi-Fi hotspot-based setup:

  * Remote ESP32 → provides the Wi-Fi connection.
  * Rover ESP32 → connects to the remote Wi-Fi.
* This provided a simpler communication architecture for the project.

## 5. Wi-Fi Configuration

* Initially, changing the Wi-Fi network required modifying the code.
* Added a Wi-Fi configuration/data-input system so network details could be configured more easily.
* This reduced the need to rewrite the main control program when changing network settings.

## 6. Rover Motor Control

* Tested forward, backward, left, and right movement.
* Faced issues where some motors moved in the opposite direction.
* Corrected motor polarity and control logic.
* Adjusted the left-front and left-rear motor configuration.

## 7. ESP32 Upload/Programming Issues

* Faced upload and connection issues while developing with different ESP boards and environments.
* Tested the boards individually and verified the correct board configuration, USB/COM connection, and GPIO assignments.
* Continued development after confirming successful ESP32 programming.

## 8. Code Development & Testing

* The project was developed through multiple iterations rather than using a single final version from the beginning.
* Individual features were tested before combining them:

  * Wi-Fi
  * Communication
  * Motor control
  * Rover movement
  * Remote control

## 9. 3D Printing & Rover Body Design

* Over the last three days, development has shifted toward planning and designing the **3D-printed models and physical enclosure** for the rover.
* Focused on determining suitable locations and dimensions for **ports, displays, switches, and other external components**.
* Currently facing challenges with the placement of ports and displays while maintaining accessibility and a practical overall design.
* Component placement also needs to account for **ESP32 connections, wiring, accessibility, and the overall rover structure**.
* The 3D model is currently being refined, and further adjustments will be made before producing the final printed parts.

## 10. Motor GPIO Reassignment

* The rover motors were not responding correctly with the previous ESP32 GPIO configuration.
* Investigated the motor-control wiring and GPIO assignments.
* Reassigned the ESP32 GPIO pins used by both motor drivers.
* Updated the rover motor-control code to match the new GPIO assignments.
* The new configuration maintains separate control of the left and right motor drivers.
* Further testing is being carried out to verify forward, reverse, left, and right movement with the new pin configuration.

## Current Status

* The rover's communication and motor-control system has gone through multiple rounds of testing and improvement.
* The project is currently focused on **finalizing the 3D-printed body design and component placement** before moving toward the final physical assembly.
